---
import Feature from './Feature.astro';
import Mark from './Mark.astro';
import Shot from './Shot.astro';
---

<section id="features" class="wrap features">
  <Feature eyebrow="The assistant · new in 0.2" title="Ask it to put on the weather radio." stacked>
    An assistant beside the inspector tunes, scans, sets the squelch, records and keeps bookmarks
    from plain requests. Open any step to see what it did. It asks before taking the radio from
    another listener, deleting a recording or starting paid transcription, and Stop ends a turn.
    Conversations stay with you across restarts.
    <Shot slot="visual" name="app-assistant" frame="none" />
  </Feature>

  <Feature eyebrow="Recording and speech" title="Every over, its own clip. Now with the words.">
    Record each transmission with half a second of audio before it starts. Add speech-to-text to
    search the words, play the matching clip, or export text and SRT. Watch a live channel for
    phrases such as “net control”. Speech sends each over’s audio to your OpenAI or
    OpenAI-compatible provider; it doesn’t run on the Mac.
    <Shot slot="visual" name="library-speech" />
  </Feature>

  <Feature eyebrow="AI on your own key" title="Choose the provider. See what it uses." flip>
    Add an Anthropic, OpenAI or OpenAI-compatible key in Settings → AI. Choose a model for the
    assistant and a supported provider for speech. Keys stay in the Mac’s login keychain. Settings
    shows what each feature sends, where it goes, and your usage: conversation and tool results for
    the assistant, audio for speech. Listening and recording need no AI key.
    <Shot slot="visual" name="settings-ai" />
  </Feature>

  <Feature eyebrow="Memories by band" title="Your CHIRP memories, organized by band.">
    Import a CHIRP export and your memories become bookmarks with their tones, offsets and notes,
    filed by band. Memories on the same frequency share a row and keep their own details. Sort by
    frequency or name, or tune straight to the built-in NOAA weather, GMRS, MURS and marine
    channels.
    <Shot slot="visual" name="sidebar-chirp-import" />
  </Feature>

  <Feature eyebrow="Tone discovery" title="Hear a repeater, learn its tone." flip>
    On an NFM channel Leyline listens under the voice for a CTCSS tone or a DCS code and shows it
    beside the bookmark’s tone, the way your radio’s menu would: PL 127.3, or DCS 023 in octal. Add
    it to the bookmark, then program your handheld.
    <div slot="visual" class="pair">
      <Shot name="inspector-tone" />
      <Shot name="inspector-dcs" />
    </div>
  </Feature>

  <Feature eyebrow="Scanning" title="Sweep a band and see what’s on it." stacked>
    Scan band lists carriers with their frequency, width, signal-to-noise and how often they were
    seen, then marks them on the band rail. A single burst reads differently from a repeater that is
    always up. A scan reports when the radio is already in use.
    <div slot="visual" class="stack">
      <Shot name="scan-2m" frame="terminal" />
      <Shot name="scan-app" />
    </div>
  </Feature>

  <Feature eyebrow="Decoding" title="APRS, weather alerts and AIS, decoded." stacked>
    Decoders share the radio with you when their channel is in range, or run on a second radio.
    Follow APRS stations as they beacon, see vessels on AIS, and set a watch that sends a macOS
    notification when a SAME alert names your county. Alerts can also go to a webhook or a shell
    command.
    <div slot="visual" class="stack">
      <div class="notif mac">
        <span class="notif-icon">
          <Mark size={20} />
        </span>
        <div class="notif-body">
          <div class="notif-top">
            <span class="notif-title">Severe Thunderstorm Warning</span>
            <span class="when">now</span>
          </div>
          <span class="where">Fairfax County, VA, until 21:45</span>
          <span class="src">SAME on NOAA WX2 · 162.400 MHz</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <Shot name="aprs-track" frame="terminal" />
      <Shot name="ais-track" frame="terminal" />
    </div>
  </Feature>
</section>

<style>
  .features {
    padding-top: var(--section-space);
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--section-space);
  }

  .mac {
    font-family: var(--font-mac);
  }

  .stack {
    width: 100%;
    margin: 0 auto;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 14px;
  }

  .stack :global(.shot) {
    max-width: none;
  }

  .pair {
    width: 100%;
    max-width: 480px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: 14px;
  }

  /* Decoding */
  .notif {
    align-self: flex-end;
    width: min(100%, 370px);
    display: flex;
    gap: 12px;
    padding: 14px;
    border-radius: 16px;
    background: rgb(40 44 48 / 92%);
    border: 1px solid #33393d;
    box-shadow: 0 24px 50px -20px rgb(0 0 0 / 90%);
  }

  .notif-icon {
    width: 36px;
    height: 36px;
    flex: none;
    border-radius: 9px;
    background: var(--bg);
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    color: var(--accent);
  }

  .notif-body {
    flex: 1;
    min-width: 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 2px;
    font-size: 13px;
  }

  .notif-top {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: 8px;
  }

  .notif-title {
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--text-bright);
  }

  .when {
    font-size: 11.5px;
    color: var(--muted-2);
  }

  .where {
    color: #d5d8da;
  }

  .src {
    color: var(--muted-2);
    font-size: 12px;
  }
</style>
